 "reading": {
  "verdict": "provisional - eWAR v2 (FIP pitching ONLY, the Statcast fielding swap was measured and REMOVED) LOSES the descriptive gate, WINS the pitcher persistence gate, and is a NULL against bWAR on team forward-looking wins; it is not ready to replace bWAR site-wide",
  "headline": "Gate 1 (team wins, 300 team-seasons): eWAR r 0.8595 vs bWAR 0.8803, zero-parameter MAE 4.306 vs 3.787. eWAR is a WORSE description of the wins a team actually banked.",
  "why_that_was_expected": "Gate 1 rewards DESCRIBING the season that happened. B-Ref prices pitchers on the runs they actually allowed, which is most of what decided the games, so a runs-allowed WAR is close to mechanically fitted to team wins. FIP deliberately throws away sequencing and balls in play, so it must fit worse in-sample. Any FIP-based WAR (fWAR included) loses this test to a runs-allowed WAR. Losing it is not evidence the pitching swap is wrong; it is the price the swap is supposed to pay.",
  "what_it_bought": "Gate 2: the FIP-priced pitching component self-correlates 0.3617 year to year against 0.1768 for the runs-allowed component (1677 pitcher-season pairs, same rows), and it predicts NEXT year's actual runs above average better than this year's actual runs above average does (0.2573 vs 0.1768). That is a 2.1x persistence gain and the entire case for a FIP basis.",
  "what_it_did_NOT_buy": "A TEAM-LEVEL forward-looking win. Measured 2026-08-04 and stated here because the opposite was briefly claimed: on team WAR in year y against wins in y+1, eWAR reads 0.2524 against bWAR 0.2481, and that gap is NOT real. Three independent reasons. (1) SCALE CONTAMINATION: 60 of 270 pairs straddle the 60-game 2020 season and correlate NEGATIVELY, dragging the pooled r down; on full-schedule pairs only (n 210) it is 0.592 vs 0.5913, a delta of 0.0006. (2) A zero-information control that SHUFFLES the per-team eWAR-minus-bWAR delta across teams produces an absolute delta at least as large 82.3% of the time, and beats bWAR outright in 27.3% of shuffles. (3) Per year-pair, eWAR wins 4 of 9 and LOSES the four most recent; the pooled edge is carried by 2016->2017 (+0.176) and the COVID pair 2020->2021 (+0.119). The pitching component's forward case is at the PITCHER level (gate 2), where it is large and survives controls. It does not survive aggregation to the team.",
  "the_component_that_was_removed": "THE STATCAST FIELDING SWAP IS NOT IN THE v2 BUILD (shipped fielding_blend_w = 0.0, so eWAR carries B-Ref rfield unchanged). This block is the COUNTERFACTUAL that removed it, not a description of what ships: the fielding_swap_only arm is built at fielding_blend_w=1.0 on purpose so it answers 'what would the swap do', which is why it still appears below. The ATTRIBUTION arms separate the two swaps. The FIP swap costs same-season r (0.8595 vs 0.8803) and BUYS next-season signal (0.2524 vs 0.2481). The Statcast fielding swap loses BOTH: same-season 0.8777 and next-season 0.2255. That is a real finding with a mechanical cause: Statcast OAA is a RANGE metric. It carries no outfield or infield ARM value, no catcher framing or blocking, no first-base scoops, no double-play pivot. B-Ref rfield does carry those. Swapping OAA in for rfield is therefore not 'a better defensive metric for the same quantity', it is a BETTER metric for a NARROWER quantity, and the value it drops is real value. data/savant_arm.json exists but is arm STRENGTH in mph from 2020 on, not runs, so it cannot close the gap without a model that does not exist yet.",
  "what_would_change_the_verdict": [
   "an arm-value and catcher-framing run model, so the fielding swap covers the same quantity rfield does instead of a subset",
   "CLOSED 2026-08-04, do NOT re-run: a BLEND of OAA and rfield rather than a replacement was built and swept. w = 0.00 / 0.25 / 0.50 / 0.75 / 1.00 gives a forward-leg delta against bWAR of +0.0006 / -0.0019 / -0.0057 / -0.0108 / -0.0173 on full-schedule pairs (and +0.0043 / +0.0009 / -0.0029 / -0.0073 / -0.0122 on the contaminated pooled set). MONOTONE DECREASING IN w ON BOTH BASES, so the 2020 contamination does not drive the ordering and no positive weight pays for itself. The blend is now the axis the decision was made on, not a candidate: w=0.0 IS v2. Re-open only if an arm-value or catcher-framing run model lands, which would change what the Statcast read MEASURES rather than how it is weighted",
   "gate 1 re-run against team RUNS ALLOWED and RUNS SCORED separately instead of wins, which separates component accuracy from the Pythagorean noise both series inherit"
  ],
  "safe_to_use_today": "The pitching component. It reproduces the known bWAR/fWAR divergences by name (Aaron Nola 2018 9.69 -> 6.15, Mike Minor 2019 7.99 -> 4.68, both toward their published fWAR), it persists 2.1x better, and it forecasts next-year run prevention better than actual run prevention does. NOTHING consumes eWAR yet. v2 already took the second of the two exits named here: the Statcast fielding swap was DROPPED (w=0.0) rather than repaired, so what remains is a one-component model. Two consumer gates have since been measured and both said no: the eFV first-6yr label swap (lane R3a) and fa_backtest Gate 1 (lane R3b). Read those before wiring eWAR into anything."
